  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A frontal boundary south of the region will \nkeep rain chances around through mid-week, especially across \nsouthern portions of the area.\n\nThe cold front that crossed through our region Sunday Night is\nexpected to stall to our south today, and remain there through\nTuesday. It continues to look like it will lift a little closer\nto our region Monday Night into Tuesday morning as a warm \nfront, but remain just south of the area. Due to the closer\nproximity, and a mid level short wave trough, mean that that\nperiod will have the highest chance for rain across our region.\nGiven that our low will be on the cool side of the front,\nshowers are going to be the main mode of any precipitation.\nHowever, isolated, embedded thunderstorms will be possible over\nDelmarva, particularly during the day on Tuesday.\n\n\nFor Wednesday into Thursday, the front should again be on the \nmove southward, lessening rain chances across the area. \n\nConfidence is high that temperatures will be below normal\nstarting Tuesday. In fact, temperatures will likely struggle to\nget out of the 60s areawide through most of this week. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A closed mid-level low ejecting from the Ohio \nValley is expected to lead to the development of a surface low \noff the Eastern Seaboard during the middle and latter parts of \nthis week. Depending on the track of the low, it could bring \nincreased rainfall chances to the region. \n\nMost of the models came into better agreement on the track and\nthe evolution of the coastal low through Thursday. Through this\ntime, the center of the low should stay far enough off shore\nthat rain chances across our region will be very low, except at\nimmediate coastal areas. However, it looks to be a deep and\nintense low, so expect a large pressure gradient, even for\ninland areas, and thus breezy to windy conditions. For coastal\nareas, we could get close to wind advisory thresholds,\nparticularly later in the day on Wednesday through Thursday.\n\nFriday into the weekend, some models have this becoming a cutoff\nlow and retrograding eastward. Notably though, this solution is\nprimarily with the deterministic, physical equation models.\nSeveral ensemble members and some of the main AI models depict \nthis low continuing northeastward. All that being said, the \nforecast, particularly the rain and wind forecast, is very \nuncertain from Friday onward.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Multiple rounds of tidal flooding are expected\nlater this week, with tidal flooding potentially beginning as\nearly as Tuesday evening. \n\nThere will be a prolonged period of onshore flow at the coast \ncontinuing through at least Thursday, and may continue through \nthe weekend. This, combined with increasing astronomical tides\nahead of the full moon on Saturday, means that multiple rounds \nof tidal flooding for the Atlantic coast, Delaware Bay, southern\nRaritan Bay, and tidal Delaware river are likely. \n\nWith the latest guidance, the earliest that we may see minor \ntidal flooding is with the Tuesday afternoon/evening high tide, \nand that appears most likely for the Atlantic coast and \nDelaware Bay from Barnegat Inlet southward. That being said, \nthere remains uncertainty in how high the tidal anomalies will \nbe by Tuesday Afternoon. \n\nThe highest confidence in tidal flooding occuring is Wednesday\nand Thursday. Through this period, we could even see moderate\ntidal flooding. \n\nOf special note with this set up, the prolonged nature of the\nonshore flow tends to result in the highest tidal anomalies on\nback bays, instead of ocean front. Consequently back bays\nincluding Barnegat Bay and Shrewsbury River in New Jersey and\nthe Indian River Bay in Delaware could see the worst impacts\nwith this tidal flooding.\n\nFriday into the weekend, there is considerably less confidence,\nas it will be highly dependent on how the coastal low evolves\nthrough this period. If the low retrogrades closer to the coast\nas some models are showing, the coastal flooding is likely to\ncontinue through the weekend.\n\nFor the Chesapeake Bay, the expected wind direction tends to\nlimit tidal flooding concerns, but it will need to be watched if\npersistent easterly flow at the mouth of the Chesapeake.\nCurrently, tidal flooding is unlikely through Wednesday Night.",
